WEBVTT

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:04.710
Imagine I’m teaching Sam to use clues in a story.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.590
Could a computer tell how Sam figured out an answer?

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:12.630
I’ll borrow three ideas from the history of tutoring and try them on the same story.

00:00:14.963 --> 00:00:17.183
Sam reads these two sentences.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.343
Water crept under the door.

00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:24.572
Mara grabbed her brother’s coat and hurried him outside.

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:27.372
I ask, “Why was Mara in a hurry?”

00:00:29.713 --> 00:00:32.293
Sam writes, “The house was flooding.”

00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:35.453
That fits. What gave Sam that idea?

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:42.558
An early teaching machine could reveal a prepared answer.

00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:45.398
Sam could check whether the two answers agree.

00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:52.597
Some computer tutors tracked each step a student took.

00:00:52.897 --> 00:00:56.517
For this story, I’d ask, “Which words were your clue?”

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:02.962
Sam underlines “Water crept under the door.”

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:05.442
Now I can see the clue Sam used.

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:12.117
A conversational tutor could ask a follow-up question.

00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.037
“Why would water on the floor make Mara hurry?”

00:01:15.337 --> 00:01:18.557
Sam explains, “They had to get out before the water rose.”

00:01:22.463 --> 00:01:24.643
In We Do, open the archive.

00:01:24.943 --> 00:01:28.043
What could each tool tell from a student’s work?
